Ladybird accepts the three prefixed text-align values, and uses physical
left and right for their block alignment in either text direction. Blink
resolves the keyword in BlockLayoutAlgorithm, WebKit resolves inline
margins in RenderBox::computeInlineDirectionMargins(), and Gecko accepts
prefixed aliases for TextAlignKeyword.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 agreed on
auto margins, asymmetric fixed margins, and safe overflow in both
directions. They also mapped left and right to the top and bottom
edges in both vertical writing modes. Firefox serializes the values
with its own prefix. Ladybird serializes them with the WebKit prefix,
as Chrome and WebKit do.

Ladybird measures float exclusions at the edge where a block aligns. It
also repositions an unchanged child when the container changes its
alignment or overflow safety. Chrome 158.0.8127.36 aligned the block
within the float-free space and honored all three overflow settings, in
BlockLayoutAlgorithm and WebkitTextAlignAndJustifySelfOffset().

Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5 kept the block at its legacy alignment in
these probes. Gecko measures float exclusions in
BlockReflowState::GetFloatAvailableSpaceWithState(). WebKit passes the
space between floats to RenderBox::computeInlineDirectionMargins().

Ladybird positions a self-aligned block using the edge its writing
mode and direction place on the containing block’s inline axis. Chrome
158.0.8127.36 matched both offsets in 200 combinations of writing
modes, directions, and widths. Blink maps the child’s edges in
WebkitTextAlignAndJustifySelfOffset(). Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5
used the prefixed parent alignment throughout these cases, in Gecko’s
ReflowInput::CalculateBlockSideMargins() and WebKit’s
RenderBox::computeInlineDirectionMargins().

For baseline, Ladybird and Chrome use the container’s start edge even
when the child has the opposite direction. CSS Align specifies safe
self-start. With a 100px RTL child in a 200px LTR block, changing the
parent alignment from left to center to right gave offsets of 0, 50,
and 100px in Firefox and WebKit, and 0px throughout in Chrome.

Ladybird checks the aligned border box against each float before it
accepts the block position. A 60px child with a 150px right margin in
a 200px RTL block cleared a 30px left float in Chrome 158.0.8127.36,
Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5. An 80px margin fit beside the float.

Blink checks the final offset in LayoutNewFormattingContext(). Gecko
checks the space in FloatAvoidingBlockFitsInAvailSpace(), and WebKit
chooses the block position in RenderBlockFlow::clearFloatsIfNeeded().
